20. Response Coordination Team
20.1 When matters related to the Policy lead to the formation of a Response Coordination Team
(RCT), the RCT will, as appropriate:
(a) determine the level of risk;
(b) coordinate support and services;
(c) consider recommendations for Interim Measures;
(d) consider alternative or additional policies or processes;
(e) provide advice to EQHR on whether a Voluntary Process is appropriate; and
(f) provide advice to EQHR under sections 15 and 17.
20.2 The RCT will include individuals based on the nature of the concern or Report. Usually an RCT
will include a delegate of the Administrative Authority and where appropriate representatives of
units that have responsibility related to the Policy (for a list of units, see Appendix A – Unit Roles
and Responsibilities).

APPENDIX F
Statement on Process Participant Rights
Procedural Authority: President Effective Date: July 1, 2025
Procedural Officer: University Secretary Supersedes: January 2021
Parent Policy: Sexualized Violence Prevention and Last Editorial Change: n/a
Response Policy (GV0245)
F1. Purpose
F1.1 The purpose of this statement is to list the rights that the Survivor, Complainant, Person Alleged
to Have Caused Harm, Respondent and witnesses can expect to have under the Sexualized
Violence Prevention and Response Policy (“the Policy”) and its associated procedures.
F2. Definitions
F2.1 The definitions in the Policy apply to these procedures.
F3. Confidentiality
F3.1 Section 24 of the Policy and Appendix G – Statement on Confidentiality and Privacy apply to
these procedures.
F4. Survivor/Complainant Rights
F4.1 A Survivor or a Complainant has the following rights during any policy process:
(a) to be treated with fairness, dignity, and respect;
(b) to Trauma-informed, non-judgmental and empathetic communications, including not to be
blamed for experiencing Sexualized Violence;
(c) to be given access to available support and resources throughout the process;
(d) to timely assistance with safety planning;
(e) to timely information about referrals to available on- and off-campus support services and
resources;
(f) to have the process explained to them in an accessible manner, including the possible
outcomes;
(g) to have their personal information kept confidential (except when disclosing it is required
by law or University policy);
(h) to be informed that any information collected may be disclosed in criminal or civil
proceedings;
(i) to be accompanied by a support person (see B6 in Appendix B – Support Procedures);
(j) to legal representation (at the Complainant’s cost);
(k) to seek independent consultation at any point from the University Ombudsperson;
(l) if the Complainant is a unionized employee, to seek support, consultation, and
representation by their union; and
(m) right to change your mind and request a different process (in some cases a process will
continue regardless as per Policy section 15).
F4.2 A Complainant has the following additional rights once a Report has been filed and/or an
investigation has been initiated:
(a) to share their experience, and to respond to other participants’ information;
(b) to decline to participate in aspects of the investigation;
(c) to request an appeal of the Director’s decision not to investigate and receive written notice
of the outcome;
(d) to regular updates on the progress of the investigation; and
(e) to inquire about restrictions that the University imposes on the Respondent and, in
compelling circumstances, be informed if those restrictions directly affect the
Complainant’s health and/or safety.
F5. Person Alleged to Have Caused Harm/Respondent Rights
F5.1 A Person Alleged to Have Caused Harm or a Respondent has the following rights during any
policy process:
(a) to be treated with fairness, dignity, and respect;
(b) to Trauma-informed, non-judgmental and empathetic communications;
(c) to be given access to available support and resources throughout the process;
(d) to timely assistance with safety planning;
(e) to timely information about referrals to available on- and off-campus support services and
resources;
(f) to be presumed to have not violated this policy until a fair investigation is completed;
(g) to have the process explained to them in an accessible manner, including the possible
outcomes;
(h) to have their personal information kept confidential (except when disclosing it is required
by law or University policy);
(i) to be informed that any information collected may be disclosed in criminal or civil
proceedings;
(j) to be accompanied by a support person (see B6 in Appendix B – Support Procedures);
(k) to legal representation (at the Respondent’s cost);
(l) to seek independent consultation at any point from the University Ombudsperson;
(m) if the Respondent is a unionized employee, to seek support, consultation, and
representation by their union; and
(n) to decline to participate (the process may still proceed and for Individuals With Supervisory
Responsibilities a decision to not participate may lead to EQHR informing their direct
supervisor as per Policy section 22.4).
F5.2 A Respondent has the following additional rights once a Report has been filed and/or an
investigation has been initiated:
(a) to receive timely notice and information about the allegations against them;
(b) to share their experience, and to respond to other participants’ information;
(c) to written notice about the outcome of an investigation and any sanctions; and
(d) a procedurally fair investigation (in accordance with collective agreement provisions,
where applicable).
F6. Witness Rights
F6.1 A witness has the following rights once they have been notified that they are a witness in an
investigation by EQHR or an investigator:
(a) to be treated with fairness, dignity, and respect;
(b) to Trauma-informed, non-judgmental and empathetic communications;
(c) to be given access to available support and resources throughout the process;
(d) to timely assistance with safety planning;
(e) to timely information about referrals to available on- and off-campus support services and
resources;
(f) to have the process explained to them in an accessible manner, including the possible
outcomes;
(g) to have their personal information kept confidential (except when disclosing it is required
by law or University policy);
(h) to be informed that any information collected may be disclosed in criminal or civil
proceedings;
(i) to be accompanied by a support person (see B6 in Appendix B – Support Procedures);
(j) to legal representation (at the witness’ cost);
(k) to seek independent consultation at any point from the University Ombudsperson;
(l) if the witness is a unionized employee, to seek support, consultation, and representation
by their union; and
(m) to decline to participate (for Individuals With Supervisory Responsibilities a decision to not
participate may lead to EQHR informing their direct supervisor as per Policy section 22.4);
and
(n) to share their experience and to respond to other participants’.
